# Line endings — LF everywhere (#888).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# Without it, whether a diff is readable depends on which machine last touched
# the file. The repo had drifted into storing some blobs CRLF and some LF —
# `MatchGameView.tsx`, `RackGameView.tsx` and `StrokeGameView.tsx` were CRLF
# while `NonGolfGameView.tsx` and most of `src/lib` were LF — so an editor that
# writes LF rewrote every line of the CRLF ones. A 102-line change to
# `StrokeGameView.tsx` arrived as a 2,640-line diff, twice in one session, which
# makes review impossible and `git blame` useless afterward.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs binary; `eol=lf` normalises those files to
# LF both in the repository AND in the working tree, so there is one answer
# rather than a per-machine one. Nothing here requires CRLF: the repo has no
# `.sh`, `.bat`, `.ps1` or `.cmd`, and its one shebang file
# (`scripts/repair-ghost-orphans.mjs`) needs LF — a CRLF shebang is a broken
# interpreter line.
#
# The renormalisation of the existing tree is a SEPARATE commit, so the noisy one
# is identifiable in history and can be skipped with `git blame --ignore-rev`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never normalised, never diffed as text. Listed explicitly rather than
# left to detection, because a mis-detected binary is corrupted by normalisation
# rather than merely displayed oddly.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
